{"kGD":{"title":"Gradient descent","mc":"#15803d","body":"<div class=\"m-meta\"><p class=\"role\">Repeat <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>←</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta-\\eta\\,\\nabla L(\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">θ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">←</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">θ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">η</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∇</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">θ</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> to lower the loss step by step.</p><div class=\"where\">📍 Location: <a data-go=\"T1\">App overview</a> › <a data-go=\"T2\">Agent</a> › <a data-go=\"T3\">LLM</a> › <a data-go=\"N\">Neural Network Basics</a> › <a data-go=\"K4\">Math · Optimization</a> › <b>Gradient descent</b></div><span class=\"status no\">📐 Math foundation: not a component, but the rule behind the computation</span></div><div class=\"tryit\"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class=\"btn primary\" data-jump=\"labGD\" data-lv=\"K4\">🧪 Learning rate and convergence (Optimization)</button></div><div class=\"m-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Card sections\"><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t0\" aria-controls=\"mt@p0\" aria-selected=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\">🎯 What it does</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t1\" aria-controls=\"mt@p1\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">▶ How to use</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t2\" aria-controls=\"mt@p2\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">🧠 Why</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t3\" aria-controls=\"mt@p3\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p0\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t0\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"sec\"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>Almost every neural network is trained with a variant of it (SGD, momentum, Adam).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p1\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t1\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec\" style=\"background:#f0f9ff\"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>Backpropagation computes the gradient; the learning rate η sets the step size.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p2\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t2\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec why\"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Among all small steps of the same length, the −∇L direction lowers the loss fastest (proved on this stop with Cauchy–Schwarz).</p></div><div class=\"sec eff\"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>As long as the step is small enough and the gradient is non-zero, every step lowers the loss.</p></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p3\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t3\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><p class=\"lim\">⚠️ Limits / notes: It only heads to a nearby low point, with no guarantee of the global minimum.</p></div><!--m2pi--><div class=\"sn\"><p class=\"sn-h\"><span>🔤 Symbols used here</span><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-all\" data-sym=\"*\">📖 All symbols</button></p><ul class=\"sn-l\"><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"theta\">θ</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “theta”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">all of the model’s “knobs” (parameters) together</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"gets\">←</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “becomes”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">Work out the right side, then store it on the left, replacing the old value, like rubbing out a scoreboard and writing the new score.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"eta\">η</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “eta”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the learning rate: how big each step is</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"mul\">□□</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: no word: side by side means times</span><span class=\"sn-m\">Two things written right next to each other, with no sign between them, are multiplied: a number right before a letter means that number times it; two brackets side by side are multiplied.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"nabla\">∇</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “nabla” or “del”: the gradient</span><span class=\"sn-m\">The “gradient”: how fast things change in each input direction, put in a list. It points the steepest way uphill; training takes a small step the opposite way so the loss goes down.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>L</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “L”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the loss: how wrong the model is (the “height of the hill”)</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"fx\">□( )</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “… of …”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">What comes out when you put the thing in brackets into the “machine” (function) named in front. Like a vending machine: put something in, get something out.</span></li></ul></div><!--/m2pi-->"},"kLR":{"title":"Learning rate & convergence","mc":"#15803d","body":"<div class=\"m-meta\"><p class=\"role\">With too small a step <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">η</span></span></span></span> progress is slow; too large and it oscillates or diverges.</p><div class=\"where\">📍 Location: <a data-go=\"T1\">App overview</a> › <a data-go=\"T2\">Agent</a> › <a data-go=\"T3\">LLM</a> › <a data-go=\"N\">Neural Network Basics</a> › <a data-go=\"K4\">Math · Optimization</a> › <b>Learning rate &amp; convergence</b></div><span class=\"status no\">📐 Math foundation: not a component, but the rule behind the computation</span></div><div class=\"tryit\"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class=\"btn primary\" data-jump=\"labGD\" data-lv=\"K4\">🧪 Learning rate and convergence (Optimization)</button></div><div class=\"m-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Card sections\"><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t0\" aria-controls=\"mt@p0\" aria-selected=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\">🎯 What it does</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t1\" aria-controls=\"mt@p1\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">▶ How to use</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t2\" aria-controls=\"mt@p2\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">🧠 Why</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t3\" aria-controls=\"mt@p3\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p0\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t0\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"sec\"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>For the quadratic <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\tfrac{a}{2}x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.1591em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6954em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">a</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> it converges if and only if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\eta&lt;2/a</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6835em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7335em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">η</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span></span></span> (proved on this stop).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p1\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t1\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec\" style=\"background:#f0f9ff\"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>Real training often warms the learning rate up first, then decays it slowly.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p2\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t2\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec why\"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The higher the curvature (larger a), the smaller the allowed step; in many dimensions the steepest direction sets the limit.</p></div><div class=\"sec eff\"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>You can check it in the lab: drag η past 2/a and the path diverges at once.</p></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p3\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t3\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><p class=\"lim\">⚠️ Limits / notes: Real losses are not quadratic; this holds only under the local approximation.</p></div><!--m2pi--><div class=\"sn\"><p class=\"sn-h\"><span>🔤 Symbols used here</span><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-all\" data-sym=\"*\">📖 All symbols</button></p><ul class=\"sn-l\"><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"eta\">η</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “eta”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the learning rate: how big each step is</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>f</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “f”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the bowl-shaped valley: the height at position x</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"fx\">□( )</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “… of …”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">What comes out when you put the thing in brackets into the “machine” (function) named in front. Like a vending machine: put something in, get something out.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>x</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “x”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">where you are standing now (the bottom of the bowl is at 0)</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"frac\">▭⁄▭</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “over” (the fraction bar)</span><span class=\"sn-m\">The top is divided by the bottom. ½ is 1 divided by 2, which is one half.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>a</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “a”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">how steep the bowl is (a number above 0)</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"mul\">□□</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: no word: side by side means times</span><span class=\"sn-m\">Two things written right next to each other, with no sign between them, are multiplied: a number right before a letter means that number times it; two brackets side by side are multiplied.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"sup\">□²</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “to the power” (the small raised mark)</span><span class=\"sn-m\">A small raised number says how many times to multiply a thing by itself: 3² = 3×3 = 9, 2³ = 2×2×2 = 8. A negative power means “flip it over”: 2⁻¹ = ½.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"lt\">&lt;</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “less than”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">The left side is smaller: 2 &lt; 5.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"slash\">/</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “divided by” (slash)</span><span class=\"sn-m\">Another way to write division: 6/3 means 6÷3 = 2.</span></li></ul></div><!--/m2pi-->"},"kSGD":{"title":"Stochastic gradient (mini-batch)","mc":"#15803d","body":"<div class=\"m-meta\"><p class=\"role\">Each step estimates the gradient from a small batch of examples instead of all the data.</p><div class=\"where\">📍 Location: <a data-go=\"T1\">App overview</a> › <a data-go=\"T2\">Agent</a> › <a data-go=\"T3\">LLM</a> › <a data-go=\"N\">Neural Network Basics</a> › <a data-go=\"K4\">Math · Optimization</a> › <b>Stochastic gradient (mini-batch)</b></div><span class=\"status no\">📐 Math foundation: not a component, but the rule behind the computation</span></div><div class=\"m-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Card sections\"><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t0\" aria-controls=\"mt@p0\" aria-selected=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\">🎯 What it does</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t1\" aria-controls=\"mt@p1\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">▶ How to use</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t2\" aria-controls=\"mt@p2\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">🧠 Why</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t3\" aria-controls=\"mt@p3\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p0\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t0\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"sec\"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>With uniform random sampling the mini-batch gradient’s expectation equals the full gradient (unbiased).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p1\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t1\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec\" style=\"background:#f0f9ff\"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>The larger the batch B, the steadier the estimate: with independent sampling the variance shrinks as 1/B.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p2\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t2\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec why\"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The total loss is the average of per-example losses, and the gradient is linear, so the gradient of the average is the average of the gradients.</p></div><div class=\"sec eff\"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>It gets a roughly right step at a tiny cost, which is much faster overall than full-batch gradients.</p></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p3\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t3\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><p class=\"lim\">⚠️ Limits / notes: The noise makes the path jittery; the learning rate is usually decayed during training.</p></div>"},"kAdam":{"title":"Momentum & Adam","mc":"#15803d","body":"<div class=\"m-meta\"><p class=\"role\">Momentum accumulates past gradients; Adam also scales each parameter’s step by the size of its gradients.</p><div class=\"where\">📍 Location: <a data-go=\"T1\">App overview</a> › <a data-go=\"T2\">Agent</a> › <a data-go=\"T3\">LLM</a> › <a data-go=\"N\">Neural Network Basics</a> › <a data-go=\"K4\">Math · Optimization</a> › <b>Momentum &amp; Adam</b></div><span class=\"status no\">📐 Math foundation: not a component, but the rule behind the computation</span></div><div class=\"m-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Card sections\"><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t0\" aria-controls=\"mt@p0\" aria-selected=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\">🎯 What it does</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t1\" aria-controls=\"mt@p1\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">▶ How to use</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t2\" aria-controls=\"mt@p2\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">🧠 Why</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t3\" aria-controls=\"mt@p3\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p0\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t0\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"sec\"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>Defaults given in the Adam paper: α = 0.001, β₁ = 0.9, β₂ = 0.999, ε = 10⁻⁸.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p1\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t1\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec\" style=\"background:#f0f9ff\"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>LLM training commonly uses AdamW, which applies weight decay separately from the gradient.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p2\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t2\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec why\"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Gradient sizes can differ by orders of magnitude between parameters; one learning rate cannot suit them all.</p></div><div class=\"sec eff\"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Bias correction (dividing by 1 − βᵗ) keeps the early estimates from being too small (derived on this stop).</p></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p3\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t3\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><p class=\"lim\">⚠️ Limits / notes: Adam stores two extra numbers per parameter (m and v), which costs memory.</p></div>"}}
